<%- include('header', { pageTitle: 'Privacy' }) %>
<section class="narrow prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Last updated 12 September 2026</p>

    <p>
        <%= brand %> is run by a private individual in Hungary. This page
        describes what it stores, why, and how to get rid of it.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Your email address</strong> — to log you in, confirm your account and reset your password. It is never shown publicly and never shared.</li>
        <li><strong>Your display name and handle</strong> — both appear on your public page if you publish a list.</li>
        <li><strong>Your password</strong> — only as a bcrypt hash. The original is never written down and cannot be recovered.</li>
        <li><strong>Your adventures</strong> — names, types, dates, descriptions, countries, and any place names or coordinates you enter.</li>
        <li><strong>Your photos</strong> — re-encoded on upload. Location and camera metadata are removed in the process and the original file is discarded.</li>
        <li><strong>Session cookies</strong> — one cookie, <code>bl.sid</code>, so you stay logged in. There is no analytics, no advertising and no third-party tracking of any kind.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is public</h2>
    <p>
        Lists are private when created. If you set one to public or share it by
        link, these become visible to anyone who opens it: the adventure's name,
        type, date, description, <strong>country</strong>, and photos.
    </p>
    <p>
        These stay private in every case: your email address, the free-text
        place name, and the exact latitude and longitude. Adventures you have
        not marked as done are also withheld unless you explicitly opt them in.
    </p>

    <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
    <p>
        The service runs on hardware owned by its operator. Two third parties
        are involved: <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/">Cloudflare</a>,
        which carries traffic to the site, and
        <a href="https://resend.com/">Resend</a>, which delivers confirmation
        and password-reset email and therefore processes your email address.
        Nothing is sold, and nothing is shared for advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
        Under the GDPR you may see your data, take it elsewhere, correct it or
        delete it. The first two are self-service: <a href="<%= u('/settings') %>">Settings</a>
        has an export that gives you everything as a JSON file, and a delete
        button that removes your account, lists, adventures and photos
        immediately and irreversibly. No request or waiting period.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention</h2>
    <p>
        Data is kept until you delete it. Verification and reset tokens expire
        automatically after 24 hours and 1 hour respectively. Nightly backups
        are kept for 30 days on the same machine that runs the service and are
        not separately encrypted, so a deleted account may persist in a backup
        for up to that long before ageing out.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
        <% if (contactEmail) { %>
            Questions or complaints: <a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>"><%= contactEmail %></a>.
        <% } else { %>
            Questions or complaints: open an issue on
            <a href="https://github.com/palosferi/bucketlist">the GitHub repository</a>.
        <% } %>
        You also have the right to complain to the Hungarian data protection
        authority (NAIH).
    </p>
</section>
<%- include('footer') %>
